1. What this crate is called, measured
   module_path!() at the root        = pkg_crate_kata
   module_path!() in product         = pkg_crate_kata::product
   module_path!() in product::category = pkg_crate_kata::product::category
   Under bare rustc the crate is named after the file: pkg_crate_kata.rs
   gives pkg_crate_kata. Under Cargo a bin target is named after its file
   too (src/bin/report.rs -> report), except src/main.rs, which takes the
   package's name, and src/lib.rs, which takes it with `-` turned into `_`.

2. Where `crate::` points, from five places in one package
   src/lib.rs               crate:: is lib.rs; `use my_package::X` is not
                            available (a crate has no name for itself)
   src/main.rs              crate:: is main.rs itself, a second crate;
                            the library is `use my_package::X`
   src/bin/tool.rs          crate:: is tool.rs, a third crate; it sees the
                            library by name and main.rs not at all
   #[cfg(test)] mod tests   inside lib.rs: crate:: is lib.rs, super:: is the
                            enclosing module, private items included
   tests/it.rs              its own crate, like a binary: `use my_package::X`,
                            pub items only

3. The trap, from the page's demo
   shared code in src/main.rs and `crate::shared::greeting()` in src/bin/other.rs
   -> E0433: could not find `shared` in the crate root
   the fix is a src/lib.rs: every binary of the package can `use` it by name
